<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_current-loops" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Current Loops</title>

	<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section were designed to be implemented in an interactive studio classroom, after students have completed the previous sections in the chapter.  They take about two hours.
	</p>
  </assemblage>

<subsubsection>
	<title>Activities</title>
  </subsubsection>

<activity>
	<title>Magnetic Dipoles</title>
	<introduction>
		<p>
	
	</p>
	<p>
		For each of the current loops shown below:
	</p>
	<figure xml:id="fig_3loops">
			<title>Three Loops Figure</title>
				<caption>Three current loops.</caption>
				<image source="loop-magnets.png" width = "50%" ><description>Three current loops, from top to bottom: clockwise when viewed from above, counterclockwise when viewed from above, clockwise when viewed from the right.</description></image>
			</figure>
	</introduction>
	
	<task>
			<p>
				Use the right-hand rule to determine the magnetic dipole moment of the loop.
			</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>
				Sketch and label a small bar magnet that would have the same magnetic dipole moment as the loop.
			</p>
		</task>
</activity>

<activity>
	<title>Dipole Energy</title>
	<p>
		
	</p>
	<p>
		Rank the configurations below from smallest to largest potential energy.  Check that your ranking makes sense by figuring out what the magnet is likely to do in each situation.
	</p>
	<figure xml:id="fig_4loops">
			<title>Four Loops Figure</title>
				<caption>Four magnet configuration.</caption>
				<image source="loop-magnets.png" width = "80%" ><description>Four bar magnets, each above a loop carrying clockwise current when viewed from above.  From left to right: Case A has the north end of the bar magnet down, Case B has the south end down, case C has the north end to the left, case D has the north end to the right.</description></image>
			</figure>
</activity>

<activity>
	<title>Flux Loops</title>
	<p>
		
	</p>
	<p>
		A square loop (side length <m>s</m>) in the plane of the screen is in a region of magnetic field.  Find the magnetic flux through the loop if:
		<ol>
			<li>
				<p>
					The magnetic field is a uniform <m>B_o</m> and points into the screen.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					The magnetic field is a uniform <m>B_o</m> and points to the right.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					The magnetic field points into the screen and increases linearly from <m>0</m> along the left edge to <m>B_o</m> along the right edge.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ol>
	</p>

</activity>

<activity>
	<title>The Pickup Coil</title>
	<introduction>
		<p>
	
	</p>
	<p>
		Run this <url href="beav.es/pickup-coil">simulation</url>.  Go to the Pickup Coil tab.
	</p>
	</introduction>
	
	<task>
			<p>
				Play with the simulation for a few minutes.  Record a list of observations.
			</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>
				How many different kinds of changes can you make that will cause the light bulb to light up?
			</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Consider the statement: <q>While the magnetic flux through the loop changes, there is current induced in the loop.</q>  What evidence is there to support this statement?  Can you find any evidence to refute the statement?  Can you find any evidence that suggests the statement is incomplete?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					 Identify what properties the induced current in the loop depends on.  Justify your answers with evidence from the simulation.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
</activity>

<activity>
	<title>Force on a Circuit</title>
	<introduction>
		<p>
	
	</p>
	<p>
		The square circuit below is in a region with a uniform magnetic field.
	</p>

	</introduction>
	
	<task>
			<p>
				Determine the current through each side of the square circuit.
			</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>
				Determine the direction of the magnetic force on each side of the square circuit.
			</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>
				Does the magnetic field exert a net nonzero magnetic force on the loop?  If so, what direction is the net force?
			</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>
				Does the magnetic field exert a net nonzero magnetic torque on the loop?  If so, what direction would you expect the loop to rotate (starting from rest)?
			</p>
		</task>
</activity>



<!--NO AI-->
</section>

